* cforge testbench: {{ spec.name }} on sallen_key_lp2
* All values SI base units. Generated - do not edit by hand.
{{ netlist }}
* --- supplies and stimulus -------------------------------------------------
Vcc vcc 0 DC {{ vpos | spice }}
Vee vee 0 DC {{ vneg | spice }}
Vin in 0 DC 0 AC 1
* --- analyses -------------------------------------------------------------
* .save is mandatory: in batch mode ngspice keeps no vectors without it, and
* .meas then silently has no data to work on.
.save v(in) v(out)
.ac dec {{ points_per_decade }} {{ f_start | spice }} {{ f_stop | spice }}
* --- measurements ----------------------------------------------------------
* fc_hz: the -3.01 dB corner (FALL=1 takes the first downward crossing, so a
* peaked response cannot return the rising edge of its peak instead).
.meas ac fc_hz WHEN vdb(out)=-3.01 FALL=1
* gain_db_passband: two decades below the corner, where the response is flat.
.meas ac gain_db_passband FIND vdb(out) AT={{ f_passband | spice }}
* atten_db_at_10fc: rejection one decade past the target corner.
.meas ac atten_db_at_10fc FIND vdb(out) AT={{ f_ten_fc | spice }}
* peak_db: passband peaking, 0 dB for a maximally flat response.
.meas ac peak_db MAX vdb(out)
* f0_hz and q_actual: at the pole frequency a 2nd-order low-pass is exactly
* -90 degrees and its magnitude equals Q (for unity DC gain). That identity
* holds for every Q, unlike peak-height inversion which degenerates at
* Q = 1/sqrt(2). NOTE: ngspice's vp() is in RADIANS, hence -pi/2.
.meas ac f0_hz WHEN vp(out)={{ '%.7f' % phase_minus_90_rad }}
.meas ac q_actual FIND vm(out) WHEN vp(out)={{ '%.7f' % phase_minus_90_rad }}
.end
